  "content": {
    "id": "p19-q31-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "31",
    "prompt": "Let $a,b\\in\\mathbb{N}$ and suppose that\n$$\\operatorname{GCD}(a,b)+\\operatorname{LCM}(a,b)=a+b.$$\nProve that either $a$ divides $b$ or $b$ divides $a$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Either $a\\mid b$ or $b\\mid a$.\n\nWriting $d=\\operatorname{GCD}(a,b)$ and using $d\\operatorname{LCM}(a,b)=ab$ gives $(d-a)(d-b)=0$, so $d=a$ or $d=b$.",
      "worked": "Let $d=\\operatorname{GCD}(a,b)$ and $L=\\operatorname{LCM}(a,b)$. Since $dL=ab$, multiplying the given equality by $d$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}d^2+dL&=da+db\\\\d^2+ab&=da+db\\\\d^2-da-db+ab&=0\\\\(d-a)(d-b)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $d=a$ or $d=b$. Since $d$ divides both $a$ and $b$, the first case gives $a\\mid b$ and the second gives $b\\mid a$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 68
  },
